==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|En-au-Horlicks.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Etymology 1===
The name of the drink, first sold as "Horlick's Infant and Invalids Food", is derived from the surname of its developers, {{w|Sir James Horlick, 1st Baronet|James}} (1844–1921) and {{w|William Horlick}} (1846–1936). The term was later extended as a euphemism for {{m|en|bollocks||mess; to make a mess of}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|Horlicks}}

# {{lb|en|chiefly|British}} A [[hot]] [[bedtime]] [[drink]] made with [[malted milk]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1990|author=Barbara Hanrahan|title=A Chelsea Girl|publisher=Ulverscroft Large Print Books|isbn=9780708921371
|text=When the theatres turned out, people flocked back for their '''Horlickses''' and cocoas. Because of the summer visitors, we were open till one o'clock in the morning.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2016-12-06|author=Jeanette Winterson|title=Christmas Days: 12 Stories and 12 Feasts for 12 Days|publisher=Grove/Atlantic, Inc.|isbn=9780802189745
|text=Kathy, drinking '''Horlicks''' and laughing about the impossibility of custard (she could not cook – she could not even stir), and obsessive about everything, found out for me that Dylan Thomas had invented a fantasy{{nb...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2021-03-10|author=Helen Jones|title=I Am a Real Person|publisher=AuthorHouse|isbn=9781665586986
|text=It did seem very odd though, drinking '''Horlicks''' in the middle of the day. From then on, first thing in the morning, after all meals, and last thing at night, Ruth made me a big mug of '''Horlicks'''. Every day, '''Horlicks'''. All day, '''Horlicks'''.}}
# {{lb|en|chiefly|UK|slang|euphemistic}} {{1|bollocks}}; a [[muddle]], a [[mess]], a [[hash]] or [[balls-up]].

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{rfv-sense|en}} {{lb|en|chiefly|UK|slang|euphemistic}} To make a [[mess]] of (something); to [[bollocks]].
#* {{quote-news|en|date=25 September 1993|author=Stuart Wolfendale|title=Column Eight|newspaper={{w|South China Morning Post}}|url=https://www.scmp.com/article/45313/column-eight|text=I have seen a priest massacred in charge of a Mass, experienced schoolmasters carbonated in charge of a class and felt hairdressers '''Horlicksed''' in charge of hair clippers. / On a more lasting level, it is possible for surprisingly long periods of time to be drunk in charge of a relationship.}}
#* {{quote-news|en|date=2001-06-28|author=Senan Molony|title=Inquiry a training ground for linguistic acrobatics|newspaper={{w|Irish Independent}}|url=https://www.independent.ie/irish-news/inquiry-a-training-ground-for-linguistic-acrobatics-26084956.html|passage=Mr O'Brien has admitted that he was tempted to pay £100,000 to Mr Lowry, out of his own personal generosity, because he had heard pub talk that Mr Lowry's Streamline Enterprises was "'''Horlicksed'''"[—]that's a word the Irish Independent has to use as an "intermediary" because the real word is too rude.}}
#* {{quote-newsgroup|en|date=2009-04-15|author=
alland...@live.co.uk|title=SKY News - Dead Man Walking|newsgroup=uk.media.tv.misc|url=https://groups.google.com/g/uk.media.tv.misc/c/sBdF5mhgZ74/m/PPfj1DSm0hsJ|passage=One does wonder how the history books will treat Gordon if, as it now looks, he won't even still be PM by the time the next election comes along. / Indeed, if he hadn’t '''horlicksed''' the economy with nuclear abandon would they have even mentioned him at all?}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date=23 August 2009|author={{w|Ben Dirs}}|title=Strauss's England worthy winners|work=BBC Sport|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/blogs/bendirs/2009/08/aesthetically_could_it_have_be.html|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20210109133646/https://www.bbc.co.uk/blogs/bendirs/2009/08/aesthetically_could_it_have_be.html|text=Not the groundsman's fault Australia '''horlicksed''' it all up in the space of a couple of hours on Friday.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=14 March 2022|author=Sam Philip|title=Citroen C4 PureTech 130 – long-term review|journal={{w|Top Gear (magazine)|Top Gear}}|url=https://www.topgear.com/long-term-car-reviews/citroen/c4/12-puretech-130-shine-plus-5dr-auto/report-6|text=Assuming the Citroen engineers haven’t really '''Horlicksed''' things up, which I suspect they haven’t, electric should be the neatest of fits for the comfy, untaxing C4.}}
